      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": "This P1 does not apply — I traced the full call chain and verified the shadowing is intentional and correct.\n\n**How `q_exponent_bits` actually gets bound in this PR**\n\n`apply_dequantization` / `apply_selective_dequantization` are only reached via:\n\n```cpp\n// csrc/fp_quantizer/fp_quantize_impl.cu (DEQUANT_SWITCH, applied to both kernels)\n#define DEQUANT_SWITCH(q_bits)                                                    \\\n    switch (q_bits) {                                                            \\\n    case 8: /* E4M3 */                                                            \\\n        apply_dequantization<T, mantisa_bits, 3, 4>...                            \\\n    case 6: /* E3M2 */                                                            \\\n        apply_dequantization<T, mantisa_bits, 2, 3>...                            \\\n    ...\n```\n\nwhich is invoked as:\n\n```cpp\nDEQUANT_SWITCH(q_mantisa_bits * q_exponent_bits)   // q_mantisa_bits, q_exponent_bits are the\n                                                   // SOURCE quantization format's bit widths,\n                                                   // passed in from deepspeed/ops/fp_quantizer/quantize.py\n```\n\nSo the template `q_exponent_bits` is **always** the source quantization format's exponent width (4 for E4M3, 3 for E3M2, ...), never the destination dtype's width. It is not a free parameter — there is no call site that binds it to 8 for a BF16 destination.\n\n**Why the local re-derivation is correct for the destination**\n\nInside the kernel, `q_buf`/`q_buf1` are the *destination-format* packed values (shifted by `mantisa_bits`, i.e. 7 for BF16, then repacked into `uint16_t`). The masks that read them therefore need the destination-format bit widths:\n\n```cpp\nconstexpr uint16_t _exponent_mask = ((1 << new_qeb) - 1) << q_mantisa_bits;\nconstexpr uint16_t _sign_mask = 1U << (q_mantisa_bits + new_qeb);\n```\n\nwhere `new_qeb == total_q_bits - mantisa_bits - 1 == 16 - 7 - 1 == 8` for a BF16 destination. Using the *source* `q_exponent_bits` (4) there instead would mask only 4 bits of a field that actually occupies 8 — the subsequent re-biasing (`dst_exponent - bias_q + bias_dst`, where `bias_q` is derived from the *source* format) and repacking into `dst_data[j]` with `<< (mantisa_bits - q_mantisa_bits)` shifts would then operate on a truncated/misplaced exponent field. That matches the existing (pre-PR) behavior exactly: on every CUDA version that compiles this code today (CUDA <=12, where the shadow is legal C++), the local variable was always the value in use, and the FP8-GEMM tests (`tests/unit/ops/fp_quantizer/test_fp8_gemm.py`) pass upstream with that behavior.\n\n**Net effect of the PR**\n\n- CUDA <=12: identical generated code to today (local variable was always shadowing; nothing changes).\n- CUDA 13+: compiles at all (currently a hard error). Generated code is bit-for-bit the same as the CUDA <=12 behavior above, since `constexpr` folding of `total_q_bits - mantisa_bits - 1` yields the same value the shadowed local always had.\n\nSo this is a pure build-fix, not a behavior change, and the \"mis-sized `quantized_bits`\" concern would actually introduce a behavior change relative to every release shipped so far. Happy to add a short comment to the code documenting why `new_qeb` must track the destination format (not the source `q_exponent_bits`) so this doesn't have to be re-derived next time — let me know if you'd like that added to this PR.",
      "commit_id": "c4ab93d5849cd26535521bdd0c8d535aed3e491e",
